    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Ringbrothers Mesmerizes the SEMA Show Once Again, This Time With Billie, the 650-HP Pontiac GTO\nLow-Speed Braking Fault Prompts GM EV Recall, Over 67K Vehicles Affected\n11 photos\nPhoto: Cadillac\nAs fate would have it, a Cadillac Lyriq that received a software update for an earlier brake system recall had struck a garage at low speed. That incident, reported internally on April 10, 2026, exposed another problem in the Detroit-based automaker's brake control software used by BEV3 models.\nPer the latest NHTSA filing, the largest manufacturer among Detroit's Big Three now wants 67,380 vehicles back for a second update. As you might have already guessed by now, the problem is not mechanical. It's actually software controlling the brake system's control module, where iffy code may wrongly interpret wheel movement, compromising braking performance under specific circumstances.\nThe recall population includes the aforementioned Lyriq crossover, along with the Cadillac-branded Vistiq, the Celestiq ultra-luxury liftback, and the Chevrolet division's Blazer EV . All of them feature BEV3 underpinnings, a platform shared with the Honda Prologue, Acura ZDX, the China-market Buick Electra E5, the Cadillac Optiq, and the Chevrolet brand's Equinox EV.\nReferred to as 26V717, this campaign is called N262570900 in General Motors lingo. General Motors reproduced the condition during internal testing. The problem occurs when the driver applies the brakes lightly while negotiating sharp low-speed turns. If the vehicle then crosses a bump such as an uneven transition at the end of a driveway, a momentary release and reapplication of the brake pedal causes the software to misinterpret wheel movement.\nYou can blame slip calculation for this error. Normal braking is said to return when the driver presses the no-no pedal again. Oddly specific, I know, but what did you expect from General Motors? In any case, this combination is why the problem is especially troublesome in environments that frequently involve tight steering angles and changes in elevation.\nPhoto: Cadillac Simply put, the module's software slip calculation was poorly coded. The garage incident mentioned earlier involved a 2024 model year Cadillac Lyriq that received apparently revised software as part of NHTSA recall 24V589. That earlier campaign makes the latest one particularly interesting.\nGM initially saw a few similarities, but subsequent testing showed that the newer problem involved a different combination of circumstances associated with all-wheel-drive powertrains. The product investigation opened in August 2026, and its review identified 24 field complaints in total.\nSaid complaints arrived between February 2024 and June 2026, and GM is aware of 20 minor accidents. Two reports allege minor injuries. Per the Ford Motor Company's biggest rival, the second recall's fix comes in the form of software that addresses the 24V589 condition as well. In the Lyriq's case, affected vehicles were produced for model years 2023 through 2027.\nAll of them feature Performance All-Wheel Drive, specifically regular production option code X0E. The Blazer EV, meanwhile, is affected only in SS and PPV flavors. The other two Cadillac nameplates have different coverage, with GM highlighting 2025 through 2027 models.\nCadillac and Chevy dealers will update the brake system control module software to correct the slip calculation at no charge to affected customers. The update can also be performed over the air as long as the customers accept the applicable T&Cs. In over-the-air form, the update shouldn't take more than 20 minutes to install.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ncadillac lyriq Cadillac Vistiq Cadillac Celestiq Chevrolet Blazer EV recall\nDownload: Cadillac and Chevrolet electric vehicle brake system software error recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tesla Is Pulling the Plug on the Cybertruck and the Internet Got the Party Started",
